WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.240
You would think the smell would be the&nbsp;
worst part. And don’t get me wrong,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:10.000
it was far from pleasant, to say the least.&nbsp;
That pungent odor, a stomach-turning scent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:16.400
like raw sewage. It wasn’t just dirt and decay;&nbsp;
no, it was far more disgusting than that. It had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:22.880
that awful metal tang to it, that meant when&nbsp;
the awful stench crawled its way up your nose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:29.520
it lingered, hanging at the back of your throat,&nbsp;
choking you on the sickening reek that lay thick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:34.560
in the air like a foul-smelling fog.
And yet, like I said, that was far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:39.600
from the worst part. What was? Well, even the&nbsp;
sight of what had happened to him wasn’t what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.120
made me feel unsettled. Hardly any of his&nbsp;
body was visible, covered in that writhing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.120 --> 00:00:51.840
shifting cloak. But I could see it move, each&nbsp;
tiny piece of the mass moving on its own.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:57.920
It made me imagine what it must have felt like, to&nbsp;
have all those little scratchy legs and pincers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:04.560
claws, and teeth hooking into his skin. And that,&nbsp;
the idea of what it would feel like… that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.560 --> 00:01:09.680
the worst part about seeing the Vermin God.
City life isn’t really all it’s cracked up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:15.200
to be. Ask anyone who lives in a big urban&nbsp;
area and they’ll likely tell you the same.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:21.120
Those bright lights and tall spires reaching up to&nbsp;
the clouds, they offer the promise of prosperity,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:27.760
opportunity, and even fame and fortune for those&nbsp;
who are extra naïve. But when they upped sticks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.832
packed their bags, and moved here to start a&nbsp;
new life, they realized that all along they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.832 --> 00:01:39.200
had been sold on a dream, a false fantasy&nbsp;
that is just simply too good to be true.&nbsp;

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.440
Of course, by the time they get here it’s&nbsp;
already too late, and before they know it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.320
they’re knee-deep in debt, trying to keep up with&nbsp;
paying rent on an apartment that’s barely big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:54.240
enough for one person. If you aren’t careful, a&nbsp;
big city can swallow you up, sometimes literally.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.160
Never mind just desperately trying to&nbsp;
make ends meet and keep yourself afloat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.960
there’s also plenty of danger to be found in the&nbsp;
heart of the city. It doesn’t take much for people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.960 --> 00:02:08.000
to lose themselves in the darker parts of this&nbsp;
metropolitan hell pretending it’s a haven. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:13.520
when they do, that’s usually where my job starts.
I had been working as a Private Investigator for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:18.320
coming up on a decade. My specific job title had&nbsp;
gone through a number of rebrands and different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:24.480
combinations of terms over the years; Cassius&nbsp;
Duke, Detective-for-Hire, then ‘Private Eye’,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.480 --> 00:02:29.680
and ‘Freelance Investigator’ after that. And don’t&nbsp;
fall under the same sort of assumptions that most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:35.520
people do when they hear all those terms; believe&nbsp;
me, just like the city’s promises, it rarely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:41.200
lives up to what you’d imagine it to be like. My&nbsp;
life was never high-speed chases and gunfights,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.160
solving murders, and tracking down crooks that&nbsp;
robbed banks in clown masks. That was work for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:51.200
the ‘real’ police, allegedly much higher&nbsp;
above my pay grade now that I had quit.&nbsp;

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:55.760
Most of the time I dealt with uncovering the&nbsp;
various dirty little secrets this grimy city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.760 --> 00:03:01.360
had to offer. More often than not, my job was&nbsp;
working as either a spy or enforcer that anyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:06.480
could hire. I followed around the rivals of&nbsp;
wealthy businessmen, snapping photos of them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:11.680
with my camera to get any dirt I could, that my&nbsp;
more unethical clients would probably use for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:16.880
blackmail. Or, I’d be warning stalkers to keep&nbsp;
away from whoever they were causing trouble for,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.880 --> 00:03:21.840
usually with a bit of ‘gentle encouragement’&nbsp;
in the form of some pretty stern words and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:26.160
a rap on the knuckles of sorts. It&nbsp;
was ugly work, but that suited just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.320
how ugly the underside of this whole city was.
Some of my clients were often desperate people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.320 --> 00:03:37.120
folks who the ordinary system of law and&nbsp;
order had failed. When that happened,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:41.600
when they had been turned away by the cops,&nbsp;
on the verge of losing any hope they had left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:47.840
that was usually when they turned to me for help.
Now, I didn’t work for free, of course;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:53.280
you get nothing for nothing, everything’s got its&nbsp;
price, so on and so forth. But I wasn’t looking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:59.600
extort people, especially at their lowest moments.&nbsp;
So, firm but fair, I’d charge them a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.600 --> 00:04:04.240
for my time, maybe a finder’s fee if I happened&nbsp;
to come across some dirt that was really worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:10.160
some extra cash. It was rare for me to take on a&nbsp;
job ‘pro bono’ as it were, without expecting any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:17.440
pay for my services. But when this case landed in&nbsp;
my lap, I felt obligated to do the job for free.&nbsp;

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:23.440
It was another grey day in a grey city. A knock on&nbsp;
the door interrupted me from my busy schedule of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.560
staring at the paint flaking off my ceiling. After&nbsp;
I’d called them through, the person responsible&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:34.080
for that knock came stepping cautiously into my&nbsp;
office. He told me his name was Martin Jenkins,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:38.160
but that his friends called him Marty.
“Sorry, I don’t really know how all this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:42.080
works,” Martin admitted, making no&nbsp;
attempt to mask how nervous he was.&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:46.000
“Well, why don’t you take a seat&nbsp;
first?” I sighed. “That’s a start.”&nbsp;

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:51.760
“Oh, okay,” he replied, awkwardly pulling up&nbsp;
a chair and sitting down opposite my desk.&nbsp;

00:04:51.760 --> 00:04:55.360
“So, Mr Jenkins, what can I-”
“Marty,” he interjected.&nbsp;

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:59.600
“Mr Jenkins,” I repeated. “What&nbsp;
is it that brings you here?”&nbsp;

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:05.680
“Well, someone said you might be able to help&nbsp;
with my… problem?” Martin said sheepishly.&nbsp;

00:05:05.680 --> 00:05:10.320
“And what sort of problem are you having, Mr&nbsp;
Jenkins?” I pushed for him to give me a straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.320 --> 00:05:15.200
answer; I didn’t really have a lot of patience for&nbsp;
anyone who wasted time just getting to the point.&nbsp;

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:21.280
“It’s my brother, you see. The other Mr&nbsp;
Jenkins,” he finally confessed. “He’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.280 --> 00:05:25.920
been missing, it’s actually been months now.”
“A missing person’s case is a police matter, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:30.720
sorry, but you’ll have to go to them.” As bad as&nbsp;
it felt turning the guy down at a time like this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.200
I had my profession to protect; if I wasn’t&nbsp;
careful, the cops would come down on me like a ton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.200 --> 00:05:40.400
of bricks. I’d already ruffled up some feathers&nbsp;
in that area; not long before, I had taken a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.800
case wherein I’d accidentally photographed&nbsp;
some corrupt officers accepting bribes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:49.840
Once those pictures wound up on the evening news,&nbsp;
there had been a target on my back ever since.&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:54.960
“But, Detective Duke-” Martin began to say.
“Mister Duke,” I corrected him.&nbsp;

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.280
“I’ve already tried going to the police. They&nbsp;
wouldn’t even accept it as a missing persons case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:03.520
when I explained what happened to my brother.”
“Go on,” I said, reaching for my notebook&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.520 --> 00:06:06.240
and a pen on the desk.
“I left the city a while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.240 --> 00:06:11.760
ago after my brother and I – Melvin, his&nbsp;
name’s Melvin Jenkins – we had an argument.”&nbsp;

00:06:12.320 --> 00:06:17.760
“And what was it that triggered this argument?”&nbsp;
I asked, dragging my pen across the page.&nbsp;

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:24.320
“It was… well, a lot of things,” Martin&nbsp;
sighed. “Our father died almost a year ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.320 --> 00:06:29.760
it wasn’t unexpected, but Mel took it pretty&nbsp;
hard. Then, the doctors told us the condition&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:34.880
dad had carried a chance of being hereditary.&nbsp;
So, we both went to get tested. I didn’t show&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:40.000
any signs of developing it, but Melvin did.&nbsp;
He took it as a death sentence, got very low,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:44.400
and started wasting all of our inheritance on&nbsp;
every vice he could get his hands on. Drinking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.400 --> 00:06:49.680
partying, gambling; you name it, he was doing it.”
“And this led to the argument?” I probed.&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.720
“It did. I confronted him before I left town,&nbsp;
I was mad that he’d been using all our dad left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:59.680
us for such frivolous and irresponsible things,”&nbsp;
Marvin continued his story, almost unable to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:04.400
now he’d started. “He didn’t want to hear any of&nbsp;
it, said he was going to die eventually anyway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.400 --> 00:07:09.120
so what was the point. Then, I told him he was&nbsp;
selfish, and that was the last time we spoke.”&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:12.880
“When did you notice he was missing?”
“Soon as I came back from out of town,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:17.280
he answered. “I went to the apartment –&nbsp;
dad had left us that too, along with all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:21.920
the payments still due – and I found that&nbsp;
Mel was gone. Apparently, he had bet it in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:26.560
a card game and lost badly. I had no way of&nbsp;
finding him, nobody else knew where he was.”&nbsp;

00:07:26.560 --> 00:07:29.520
“Let me guess,” I stepped in.&nbsp;
“When you went to the police,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:29.520 --> 00:07:33.920
they said he’s most likely living on the streets,&nbsp;
which would make him a lot harder to find.”&nbsp;

00:07:33.920 --> 00:07:38.480
Martin nodded solemnly, clearly feeling guilty&nbsp;
for what had happened, although from where I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:43.680
was sitting, it was his brother’s own misgivings&nbsp;
that had created this situation. I went on to ask&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.680 --> 00:07:48.320
a few more background questions, before asking&nbsp;
if Mister Jenkins could provide me with a photo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:53.760
of his missing brother. He obliged, handing&nbsp;
over a printed picture. The moment I saw it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.760 --> 00:07:58.720
I offered to take the case for free.
His brother, Melvin, I recognized him almost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.720 --> 00:08:04.400
instantly. He was in his mid, or possibly late&nbsp;
thirties, average height and build in the picture;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:09.120
far better shape and a lot cleaner than&nbsp;
when I’d seen him. But it was the same guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.120 --> 00:08:13.600
that much I was sure of, no amount of&nbsp;
unshaved stubble or disheveled clothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:19.120
could change that. I had seen him – seen&nbsp;
Melvin – outside my building a few times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:24.160
sleeping rough on the sidewalk. Every day that I&nbsp;
showed up to start another day of work, I’d drop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.160 --> 00:08:29.680
him a few dollars, anything I had in my wallet.
At first, I told myself it was for good luck, so I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.680 --> 00:08:34.320
didn’t attract any bad karma for myself. But when&nbsp;
I actually paid attention to how he was living,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:40.080
the gesture became what it should have been from&nbsp;
the start. It wasn’t about me, or sparing myself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:45.120
bad luck, it was just about trying to help out&nbsp;
someone who needed it, when I was in a position&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:50.080
to do him a small kindness at the very least.
One night, I’d just locked up my office and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.080 --> 00:08:55.040
was about to head home. As I stepped off the&nbsp;
curb, head down, eyes on my phone, a truck came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.040 --> 00:08:59.520
speeding down the street. I would have been killed&nbsp;
instantly had a hand not grabbed me and pulled my&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:05.520
back onto the sidewalk. Melvin had hauled me out&nbsp;
of harm’s way. He didn’t say anything to me, and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.520 --> 00:09:11.360
failed to do any more than just thank him verbally&nbsp;
before heading him. So, I figured I owed him one.&nbsp;

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:15.600
While I didn’t tell his brother this, it had&nbsp;
been a few months since I’d last seen Melvin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.600 --> 00:09:22.000
outside my building. I’d always assumed he’d moved&nbsp;
on, or been asked to move by an overzealous cop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:27.200
Of course, that meant figuring out where he&nbsp;
was going to be tricky, exactly the reason&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.200 --> 00:09:31.920
the police had turned Martin away when he’d&nbsp;
asked them for help locating his brother. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:37.360
I had a few connections that, even if they hadn’t&nbsp;
seen Melvin, might have at least heard something,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.360 --> 00:09:42.800
or could know someone else that had seen him.&nbsp;
It wasn’t a lead yet, but it was a start.&nbsp;

00:09:42.800 --> 00:09:46.640
Dee was the head of the city’s biggest&nbsp;
homeless shelter, and a good friend of mine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.640 --> 00:09:52.000
So, she became my first port of call. I showed&nbsp;
her the photo of Melvin, and asked if he’d come&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.000 --> 00:09:55.120
to stay at the shelter at all recently.
“Doesn’t ring a bell, I’m afraid.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.120 --> 00:09:59.120
She looked sombrely at the picture.&nbsp;
“Sorry, Duke, I wish I could be more help.”&nbsp;

00:09:59.120 --> 00:10:04.560
“Not on you,” I replied, taking the photo as she&nbsp;
handed it back to me. “You mind if I ask around?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:08.400
See if anyone here recognizes him?”
“Be my guest,” Dee said.&nbsp;

00:10:08.400 --> 00:10:12.640
I spent about an hour milling around, asking&nbsp;
a few down-on-their-luck folks if they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.640 --> 00:10:18.000
recognized the man in the photo. A few of them&nbsp;
mentioned they’d seen him around here and there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:22.880
sleeping rough in a number of different parts of&nbsp;
the city but none of them could give me anything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:28.400
concrete, no definite location I’d be able to&nbsp;
search next. That was until one of them spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:34.160
up. He was an older guy, bedabbled clothes and&nbsp;
unwashed hair, with a thick beard, who had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.400
craning his neck to try and sneak a look at&nbsp;
the photo when I’d been showing someone else.&nbsp;

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:42.160
“I’ve seen him,” the man –&nbsp;
Clarence – said matter-of-factly.&nbsp;

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:46.720
“You recognize this man?” I asked to verify.
“Said I seen him,” he repeated.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:50.320
“Looks different now though.”
“Well, that’s to be expected,” I replied.&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:53.280
“I don’t just mean he’s gotten&nbsp;
skinnier because he ain’t been eating,”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:57.360
Clarence said in retort. “I mean that he&nbsp;
don’t even look like he’s human no more.”&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.760
“What does that mean?” I questioned, looking&nbsp;
back at the bearded man in confusion.&nbsp;

00:11:01.760 --> 00:11:07.520
“That man right there stopped being a man. No idea&nbsp;
when or how it happened. But last time I saw him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:11.040
it was already too late.&nbsp;
Nowadays, he’s the Vermin God.”&nbsp;

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:16.000
I didn’t really have much idea how to respond,&nbsp;
unsure if Clarence was pulling my leg and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.000 --> 00:11:21.280
deliberately wasting my time, or if there&nbsp;
was some truth to this bizarre urban legend.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:27.200
At any rate, it was the only lead I had so far,&nbsp;
albeit a tenuous one at best. So, I pressed the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:27.200 --> 00:11:32.800
old man to tell me where he’d seen Melvin,&nbsp;
of this ‘Vermin God’ as he kept calling him.&nbsp;

00:11:32.800 --> 00:11:37.360
“There’s an abandoned warehouse down on the&nbsp;
waterfront,” Clarence had explained; I knew the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:37.360 --> 00:11:42.160
place he meant, it used to be a go-to for people&nbsp;
who were sleeping rough. “Lot of us used to stay&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:46.960
there, but that’s when the pests got bad.”
“Pests? What kind of pests?”&nbsp;

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.600
“All kinds. Rats, roaches, you name it. Then,&nbsp;
just as we were having to deal with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.600 --> 00:11:55.680
the company that owned the building sent cops&nbsp;
down to clear us out, told us it was unsafe and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:55.680 --> 00:12:01.040
we should move on. It was sitting there, empty for&nbsp;
ages, so I decided to chance it, head down there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.040 --> 00:12:06.080
and see if we could start using the place again.&nbsp;
But when I went to look… he was there. Waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.080 --> 00:12:11.760
in the dark. And he wasn’t alone, he ain’t ever&nbsp;
alone, they’re always with him, all around him.”&nbsp;

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:16.800
I chose to ignore Clarence’s attempts at unnerving&nbsp;
me, getting him to confirm once again that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.320
in fact Melvin he had seen. My next call was&nbsp;
following up on the wild story, so I hopped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.320 --> 00:12:27.840
on the next bus across town, huddled together&nbsp;
with commuters, holding on to ceiling rails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.840 --> 00:12:33.200
swaying about with every turn of the bus like we&nbsp;
were meat hanging in the back of a butcher’s van.&nbsp;

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:37.920
I walked quite a way from the bus stop towards&nbsp;
the docks. Most of the area was restricted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:37.920 --> 00:12:43.040
off-limits to members of the general public given&nbsp;
how hazardous it could be. I wasn’t concerned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:43.040 --> 00:12:47.760
in my line of work I usually had to get into&nbsp;
places I wasn’t supposed to be. Pulling a pair&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.760 --> 00:12:52.400
of wire cutters from my jacket pocket, I snipped&nbsp;
through the chain-link fence that surrounded the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:58.400
warehouse Clarence had directed me to. The place&nbsp;
wasn’t guarded, after all, it was empty. Lifting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.400 --> 00:13:02.960
up the wire mesh and slipping underneath through&nbsp;
the gap, I grabbed my flashlight and headed in.&nbsp;

00:13:02.960 --> 00:13:08.480
The warehouse was well and truly deserted,&nbsp;
previously used for storage but now just an empty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:15.040
space that was being left vacant, wasted in its&nbsp;
lack of use. All around was silence, occasionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.040 --> 00:13:19.760
broken by beads of water dripping from the&nbsp;
skylight into a puddle on the ground. Suddenly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:25.040
I heard a new noise, a crunch of something&nbsp;
being squashed under the sole of my shoe. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:29.760
lifted my foot and shone my torchlight downwards,&nbsp;
looking to see what it was I’d just stepped on.&nbsp;

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:36.480
It was a cockroach, far bigger and fatter than&nbsp;
any I’d ever seen, smashed to pieces underneath&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:42.160
my shoe. I stamped the rest of it off, a little&nbsp;
disgusted as everyone gets when they see a roach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.160 --> 00:13:47.440
dead or alive. That’s when I noticed&nbsp;
another sound, a low, dull buzzing. My&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.440 --> 00:13:52.320
initial thought was that it was electrical,&nbsp;
maybe a generator that was still running. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:57.200
started searching around for the source of the&nbsp;
sound, thinking that if I could flip the switch,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.200 --> 00:14:02.080
it might turn on some lights in here which&nbsp;
would make searching for Melvin a little easier.&nbsp;

00:14:02.080 --> 00:14:08.400
Suddenly, as I turned a corner, the buzzing sound&nbsp;
got louder. It wasn’t just closer it was… angrier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.400 --> 00:14:14.160
more intense. What was worse was that I’d&nbsp;
walked directly into a cloud of something, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.160 --> 00:14:19.600
lots of somethings. Tiny flies were whizzing&nbsp;
past my ears, the noise of their wings so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:19.600 --> 00:14:24.480
close it was almost deafening. Meanwhile,&nbsp;
even more, were zipping around my face,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:24.480 --> 00:14:29.600
landing on my skin and rubbing their little&nbsp;
germ-coated legs together. Waving my arms wildly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:29.600 --> 00:14:34.960
I tried my best to disperse the swarm, clearing a&nbsp;
few of them away as I tumbled into the next room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:34.960 --> 00:14:38.800
My flashlight clattered to the floor, and&nbsp;
I crawled over to pick it back up, blindly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.800 --> 00:14:44.720
swatting more of the insects away in the dark.
That’s when I saw him. The Vermin God.&nbsp;

00:14:44.720 --> 00:14:49.760
With no light in the room, only the beam from&nbsp;
my flashlight, I hadn’t noticed it at first.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:49.760 --> 00:14:55.200
The second the light fell over the writhing mass,&nbsp;
it shifted. As a matter of fact, it didn’t stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:55.200 --> 00:15:02.080
moving, not once. It was a seething assortment of&nbsp;
vermin, mostly black rats, their fur wet with what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:02.080 --> 00:15:07.760
I could only assume was water straight from the&nbsp;
sewer. But there was more; worms, huge spiders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:13.680
thick roaches dropping off of the huge shape as&nbsp;
it turned. It stood up, a mass of animals and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.680 --> 00:15:19.280
insects standing like a man, lumbering towards&nbsp;
me. Creatures were falling off with every step,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:19.280 --> 00:15:23.280
only to crawl back in and join the rest.
I pushed myself back on the floor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:23.280 --> 00:15:27.360
trying to get away from what I assumed to be&nbsp;
a horrific monster. Something controlling all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:27.360 --> 00:15:31.600
the rats and vermin, making them form&nbsp;
around it like a cloak of pure filth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:37.440
and decay. The stench of it as well, the foul,&nbsp;
unclean smell hanging in the air and lingering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:43.760
at the back of my throat made me want to be sick.
It was so repugnant that it kept me crawling back,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:43.760 --> 00:15:48.400
until my back was pressed up against the&nbsp;
wall, trapping me between it and the Vermin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.400 --> 00:15:54.000
God. It leaned closer, reaching out an arm,&nbsp;
covered in grime and cockroaches all crawling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:54.000 --> 00:15:59.920
over each other. I turned away and closed my eyes,&nbsp;
yelling out into the dark for anyone to come and…&nbsp;

00:15:59.920 --> 00:16:04.880
“Help,” a voice said weakly.
I looked up at it, panting and shaking in fear.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:04.880 --> 00:16:09.360
With trembling fingers, I lifted my flashlight&nbsp;
higher, right up to the head of the Vermin God.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:14.000
Beneath the mass of rats crawling over it,&nbsp;
and the bugs wriggling about below them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.000 --> 00:16:20.800
a pair of eyes glinted in my flashlight’s beam…&nbsp;
human eyes. They were eyes I’d seen before,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:20.800 --> 00:16:26.000
the same hopelessness behind them as when he&nbsp;
used to ask for change outside my building.&nbsp;

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:30.240
It was Melvin. That’s when I started to&nbsp;
see beneath the vermin coating his body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.240 --> 00:16:35.600
catching brief glimpses of his arms and chest,&nbsp;
just as one rat moved but before another slipped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:35.600 --> 00:16:41.200
in and took its place. His skin was pockmarked&nbsp;
with bites and scratches, some fresh and still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.200 --> 00:16:46.960
bleeding, others that had started to dry. Beneath&nbsp;
the shroud of crawling insects and rodents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:53.120
his face had gotten gaunt, coated in a thick&nbsp;
layer of dirt. He wasn’t controlling these things,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:57.760
if anything the vermin seemed to&nbsp;
be using him as some kind of host.&nbsp;

00:16:57.760 --> 00:17:02.480
Just as I edged a little closer to him,&nbsp;
considering the idea of reaching into the mass&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:08.160
and pulling Melvin free, one of the rats bit me.&nbsp;
I dropped my torch at the pain of the tiny needly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.160 --> 00:17:13.760
teeth piercing my skin and started to feel more of&nbsp;
them crawling up my ankles. I turned heel and ran,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.760 --> 00:17:17.200
all the way out of the warehouse.&nbsp;
By the time I was far enough away,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:23.680
the rats that had leapt onto me had crawled back&nbsp;
off, heading back inside to their Vermin God.&nbsp;

00:17:23.680 --> 00:17:28.640
Now go and check out “SCP-1025&nbsp;
ENCYCLOPEDIA OF COMMON DISEASES”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.640 --> 00:17:34.400
and “SCP-3760 LIKE A DOLL’S EYE” for more&nbsp;
sickening stories of other anomalous illnesses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:39.360
that are guaranteed to make your skin crawl…&nbsp;
or maybe that’s just the rats you’re feeling…

